OPT-1: Dense Array Joins — Performance Analysis
=================================================
Date: 2026-07-29
Config: DuckDB, JOB, query-jit, TPDE, single-run-template, no-spec

Summary: OPT-1 provides ZERO speedup and causes significant regression.
Decision: DROP this optimization. Keep the --jit-runtime-stats infrastructure.

1. Measured Results (node-based, 113 JOB queries)
-------------------------------------------------
  OPT-1 ON:   Compile=345ms  Execution=6415ms  Middleware=384ms  Total=7144ms
  OPT-1 OFF:  Compile=348ms  Execution=4604ms  Middleware=390ms  Total=5343ms
  Baseline:   Compile=349ms  Execution=4424ms  Middleware=390ms  Total=5286ms
                                                                 ------
  OPT-1 ON vs OFF:  +1811ms execution (+39%)
  OPT-1 OFF vs baseline: +57ms (~1%, within noise — no regression from code changes)
  Queries faster with OPT-1: 0 / 113
  Queries slower with OPT-1: 22 / 113

Topdown results show the same pattern:
  OPT-1 ON:  6342ms total
  OPT-1 OFF: 4957ms total  (+1385ms regression from dense probe)

2. Root Cause: Cache-Hostile Dense Array
----------------------------------------
The dense array is indexed by PK value (0..max_pk), so its size is proportional
to the TABLE's PK domain, not the number of filtered rows in the build-side HT.

  Table              Rows     max_pk    Dense Array    HT (filtered)
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
  role_type            12         12         96B        ~300B
  company_type          4          4         32B        ~100B
  keyword         134,170    134,170       1.0MB       ~24B (1 row after filter)
  title         2,528,312  2,528,312      19.3MB       ~36MB (1.5M rows)
  name          4,167,491  4,167,491      31.8MB       ~varies
  cast_info    36,244,344 36,244,344     276.5MB       ~never build side

The hash probe's working set = filtered HT entries (often <1KB after predicates).
The dense array's working set = full PK domain (up to 277MB).

For a typical JOB join like keyword='character-name-in-title':
  - HT has 1 entry after filtering → fits L1 cache → hash probe ≈ 2ns
  - Dense array has 134K slots → 1MB → L3 resident → dense probe ≈ 40ns

For title (production_year > 1990):
  - HT has ~1.5M entries → dir ≈ 24MB → L3/memory
  - Dense array has 2.5M slots → 19.3MB → L3/memory
  - Both approaches are slow, but dense array also pays:
    - memset(0, 19.3MB) ≈ 2ms per query
    - BuildDenseArray walking all HT chains ≈ 5ms per query
    - Random probe-side access into 19.3MB sparse array

3. Why the 2-5x Speedup Estimate Was Wrong
-------------------------------------------
The original estimate compared instruction counts:
  Hash probe:  Murmur hash (5 mul+xor) + dir load + bloom check + chain walk + key cmp
  Dense probe: bounds check + single array dereference

This analysis is correct for INSTRUCTION count but wrong for EXECUTION time.
Modern CPUs are latency-bound on memory access, not instruction-bound.

The hash probe on a small filtered HT (1-100 entries, <10KB):
  - Dir + entry + row all fit in L1/L2
  - Total latency: ~4ns (L1 hit) + 1ns (instructions) = 5ns per probe

The dense probe on a 19MB sparse array:
  - Random access into 19MB → L3 miss (~40ns) or DRAM miss (~100ns)
  - Total latency: 40-100ns per probe

Dense probe is 8-20x SLOWER than hash probe for typical JOB queries because
the hash probe's working set is dominated by the FILTERED build size (tiny),
while the dense array's working set is the FULL table PK domain (huge).

4. Would a Size Threshold Help?
--------------------------------
Guarding dense probe to max_pk ≤ 1000 (tiny dimension tables):
  Eligible: comp_cast_type(4), company_type(4), kind_type(7), role_type(12),
            link_type(18), info_type(113)
  Dense array size: < 1KB each
  But: HT for these tables also has < 100 entries → probe is <0.01ms
  Expected speedup: ~0ms total across all 113 queries

Guarding to max_pk ≤ 250,000 (adds medium tables):
  Adds: keyword(134K), complete_cast(135K), company_name(235K)
  But: always filtered in JOB → HT has 1-100 entries → probe already trivial
  Expected speedup: ~0ms

No threshold provides meaningful speedup for JOB.

5. Would Approach B (Direct FlatTable Access) Help?
----------------------------------------------------
Approach B: for UNFILTERED dense-PK builds, skip HT entirely and load payloads
directly from FlatTable column arrays using row = key - 1.

Problems:
  a) Unfiltered build sides in JOB are large tables (char_name 3.1M, name 4.1M).
     Random FK-value access into multi-MB FlatTable columns has the same cache
     problem as the dense array.

  b) FK columns have NO locality within PK-ordered blocks (verified by SQL):
     movie_id in cast_info spans the full 2.5M domain within each 2048-row block.
     So FlatTable column access by FK value is random, not sequential.

  c) Implementation is complex: requires changing the payload access pattern
     (per-column FlatTable loads instead of single HT row pointer).

  d) Filtered builds (the common case) can't use Approach B.

Conclusion: Approach B is also not viable for JOB.

6. When WOULD Dense Array Joins Help?
--------------------------------------
Dense arrays beat hash probes when:
  - The build side is SMALL (max_pk < L2 size / 8 ≈ 32K)
  - The build side is NOT heavily filtered (so the HT is large relative to the array)
  - The probe side is large (millions of rows)

This pattern is rare in JOB where dimension tables are either:
  - Very small (role_type 12 rows) → both approaches trivial
  - Medium but filtered to 1-10 rows → HT is tiny, hash probe is trivial

Dense arrays could help in workloads with medium unfiltered dimension tables
(1K-30K rows) probed by large fact tables — but JOB doesn't have this pattern.

7. Lessons Learned
-------------------
  - Always profile CACHE behavior, not just instruction count.
  - For join optimizations, the working set of the BUILD side determines
    probe performance. The HT only contains filtered rows; a direct-addressed
    array spans the full PK domain.
  - Small filtered builds (the common case in star-schema joins) already
    have near-optimal hash probe performance. Optimizing the probe path
    for these cases has negligible impact.
  - The bottleneck in JOB's top queries is SCAN + FILTER on large fact tables
    (cast_info 36M rows), not the join probe itself.

8. Original Analysis from CLAUDE.md (Preserved for Reference)
-------------------------------------------------------------

### What runtime info was used

The `FlatTable` for each base table carries `dense_pk` (bool) and `pk_to_row`
(int->row mapping). These are computed at storage-plan load time
(`storage_plan.cpp:365-399`). For node-based split, when a build-side table has a dense
integer PK, the query-JIT can replace the chained hash table with a direct-addressed
array: `row_ptr = array[fk_value]`.

### Verified: 20 of 21 IMDB tables have dense PKs

Confirmed by running SQL against `imdb.db`:

  Table              Rows         PK Type
  -------------------------------------------
  cast_info          36,244,344   DENSE
  movie_info         14,835,720   DENSE
  movie_keyword       4,523,930   DENSE
  name                4,167,491   DENSE
  char_name           3,140,339   DENSE
  person_info         2,963,664   DENSE
  movie_companies     2,609,129   DENSE
  title               2,528,312   DENSE
  movie_info_idx      1,380,035   DENSE
  aka_name              901,343   DENSE
  aka_title             361,472   SPARSE (max_id=377,960 != row_count)
  company_name          234,997   DENSE
  complete_cast         135,086   DENSE
  keyword               134,170   DENSE
  movie_link             29,997   DENSE
  info_type                 113   DENSE
  link_type                  18   DENSE
  role_type                  12   DENSE
  kind_type                   7   DENSE
  comp_cast_type              4   DENSE
  company_type                4   DENSE

`aka_title` uses `pk_to_row` fallback (sparse PK); all others use `row = key - 1`.

### Original speedup estimate and why it was wrong

The estimate claimed 2-5x on join-heavy queries (16b, 8c, 7c, 10c) by comparing
instruction counts: hash probe requires Murmur hash (5 mul+xor), directory load,
bloom-tag check, chain walk with pointer chasing, key comparison. Dense probe
requires just bounds check + single array dereference.

This analysis was correct for instruction count but wrong for execution time because
modern CPUs are memory-latency-bound, not instruction-bound. See section 3 above.

### Implementation approach (Approach A — dense array as HT replacement)

Build step ran as-is, then `qjit_ht_build_dense()` populated
`dense_array[pk_value] = row_ptr` from HT entries. Probe emitted a runtime branch:
if `dense_arrays[ht_id] != null`, do direct array lookup; else fall through to
chained HT probe. Same row layout, so all downstream payload access worked unchanged.
Compatible with all cache modes.

Both DuckDB and PostgreSQL adapters were updated.

Files modified:
  include/util/param_config.h — bool jit_runtime_stats = true
  src/util/param_config.cpp — --jit-runtime-stats / --no-jit-runtime-stats
  include/qjit/query_jit_abi.h — QjitUserData + qjit_ht_build_dense() ABI
  include/qjit/query_jit_runtime.h — QjitHashTable::BuildDenseArray()
  src/qjit/query_jit_runtime.cpp — BuildDenseArray + qjit_ht_build_dense impl
  include/qjit/query_jit_executor.h — Run() signature with dense params
  src/qjit/query_jit_executor.cpp — allocate dense arrays, pack into QjitUserData
  include/adapters/duckdb_adapter.h — dense_flags/dense_max_pk in QjitCompiled
  src/adapters/duckdb_adapter.cpp — detection, symbol registration, Run() calls
  include/adapters/postgres_adapter.h — dense_flags/dense_max_pk in QjitCompiled
  src/adapters/postgres_adapter.cpp — detection, symbol registration, Run() calls
  src/split/ir_query_splitter.cpp — wire SetJitRuntimeStats for both adapters
  src/jit/ir_to_llvm.cpp — QjitUserDataTy, ht_build_dense_fn call, dense probe codegen
  measure/measure_breakdown_time_aqp.sh — 14th arg runtime_stats (on/off)

### Kernel comparison

The kernel uses `row = key - 1` (dense PK) to directly access FlatTable columns
with NO intermediate array (`sub_query_plan.cpp:1555-1568`). This avoids the memset
and BuildDenseArray costs, but still suffers from random access into multi-MB FlatTable
column arrays when FK values have no locality. The kernel's approach works because it
also uses CSR and inverted indexes to avoid scanning large tables entirely — the dense
PK lookup is just one piece of its join strategy.

### Verified assumptions (relevant to OPT-1)

1. dense_pk condition: PKs must be exactly [1, row_count], checked at
   storage_plan.cpp:376-389. Does NOT handle min_id != 1.
2. pk_to_row is allocated for non-dense tables (storage_plan.cpp:391-398).
3. Usage: only in KernelJoinStep::use_direct_pk (sub_query_plan.cpp:1555-1568).
   Zero usage in src/qjit/ or src/jit/.
4. Cache key does NOT include HT size or cardinality. Runtime-conditional code
   (dense probe branch) works with all cache modes including jit-cache=full.

=================================================================
OPT-3: Probe-Side Prefetching — Performance Analysis
=================================================================
Date: 2026-07-31
Config: DuckDB, JOB, query-jit, TPDE, single-run-template, no-spec

Summary: OPT-3 is NET NEGATIVE across all 113 JOB queries. Helps only 16b
(-11ms) and 8c (-10ms), hurts 50-58 queries (+353-380ms total). REVERTED.

Full benchmark results (all 113 queries, TPDE, cache-tmpl):
  Node-based PF OFF:  5275.8ms   Node-based PF ON (dist=24):  5673.4ms  (+7.5%)
  Topdown PF OFF:     4919.1ms   Topdown PF ON (dist=24):     5279.9ms  (+7.3%)

Root cause: the per-row conditional branch (pf_go = pf_in_bounds AND pf_any)
adds ~2ns overhead per row in TPDE-compiled code. TPDE cannot hoist the
loop-invariant pf_any check out of the row loop. With 36M rows / 12 workers,
this is ~6ms per cast_info step. The overhead exceeds the prefetch benefit
for all but 2 queries with 8M-slot HT directories (16b, 8c).

Also tested with dist=12: 5809ms (still +6.9% regression vs OFF).
Also tested with threshold=1M (1<<20): 5660ms (still +4.2% regression).
The regression persists even with a high threshold because the pf_need
prologue + pf_go branch are still emitted and executed per row.

LLVM cache-full: prefetch is also net negative (+61ms), just less severe
because LLVM O2 can partially hoist/simplify the branch.

Code was reverted from src/jit/ir_to_llvm.cpp (CompileQuerySteps).

Original single-query analysis (16b only) below — kept for reference:

1. Measured Results (16b, 8 iterations, skip iter 0 warmup)
-----------------------------------------------------------
  WITH prefetch:    median total=210.4ms  bottleneck=195.0ms
  WITHOUT prefetch: median total=221.7ms  bottleneck=206.8ms
  Speedup: 5.7% on bottleneck, 5.1% total (11.3ms saved)

2. Key Findings from Tracing
-----------------------------
  - 93% of 16b execution is ONE subquery: cast_info (36M rows) probing 5 HTs
  - Guard pass rate: 97-100% (guard ranges span nearly full FK domain)
    movie_id  guard [318, 2525771] vs domain [1, 2528312] = 99.9% pass
    person_id guard [4, 4061913]   vs domain [1, 4167491] = 97.5% pass
  - HT sizes: 42K, 68K, 2.8M, 3.7M, 3.7M entries
  - HT dir sizes: 1MB, 2MB, 64MB, 64MB, 64MB
  - Membership pre-probe: only fires for HTs <64K entries (1 of 5)
  - perf stat: 0.62 IPC, 46.7% cache miss rate, 25% LLC miss rate

3. Approach Selection
---------------------
  (a) Batch-probe phase: highest benefit, highest complexity. Rejected.
  (b) Simple row-ahead prefetch: CHOSEN. At row i, prefetch
      dir[hash(key[i+D]) & mask] for each probe HT with >32K entries.
      NumEntries() check hoisted to prologue (loop-invariant).
  (c) Prefetch during guard: only helps first probe. Rejected.

  Option (b) chosen because 99%+ guard pass rate means <1% wasted prefetches,
  and it covers all probes in multi-probe steps.

4. Implementation Details
-------------------------
  - Prologue: compute pf_need[ht_id] = (NumEntries(ht) > 32K) once per step
  - bb_body: if (pf_in_bounds && any_pf_need), unconditionally prefetch all
    eligible HTs (no per-probe branch in hot loop)
  - Prefetch locality: NTA (non-temporal, don't pollute higher cache)
  - Distance: prefetch_entry_distance_ (default 24)
  - File: src/jit/ir_to_llvm.cpp, CompileQuerySteps

5. Why Speedup Is Modest (5% not 30%)
--------------------------------------
  - Multi-worker parallelism (6 workers on 12MB L3): the combined working set
    of all workers exceeds L3 (6 × ~6MB per morsel for 5 HTs = 36MB > 12MB),
    but within a single morsel the HW prefetcher partially helps sequential
    column scans.
  - Prefetch distance (24 rows × ~6ns hash = 144ns look-ahead) can hide L3
    misses (~10ns) but not full DRAM misses (~80ns). The 3 DRAM-resident HTs
    (64MB dir) benefit less from shallow prefetch.
  - The guard/filter/probe pipeline has limited slack between prefetch issue
    and probe consumption for back-to-back probes within the same row.

