OPT-4: Multi-Key Block Skip — Analysis (DROPPED)
=================================================================
Date: 2026-07-31

Summary: Block skip on FK columns provides ZERO benefit for JOB.
Decision: DROP this optimization.

1. FK Column Block-Level Locality (verified by SQL)
---------------------------------------------------
  Table: cast_info (36M rows, 17698 blocks of 2048, ordered by id)

  Column        avg_span     domain       locality
  movie_id      2,506,689    2,525,975    99.2% -> NO LOCALITY
  person_id     1,127,639    4,061,926    27.8% -> MODERATE
  role_id       0            11           0%    -> CONSTANT/BLOCK

  Table: movie_companies (2.6M rows, 1274 blocks, ordered by id)

  Column        avg_span     domain       locality
  movie_id      85,728       2,525,745    3.4%  -> MODERATE
  company_id    88,658       234,997      37.7% -> MODERATE

2. Why Block Skip Provides Zero Benefit
---------------------------------------
  Block skip condition: skip block if block_max < guard_lo OR block_min > guard_hi.

  For cast_info probing in 16b:
  - movie_id: each block spans 99.2% of domain → always overlaps guard range
  - person_id: moderate locality but guard range [4, 4061913] = 97.5% of domain
    → block_min > 4061913 never happens for any block
  - Guard ranges are wide because accumulated HTs contain millions of entries
    spanning the full join key domain

  For movie_companies probing:
  - movie_id: moderate locality (3.4%) but guard range covers near-full domain
  - company_id: moderate locality but guard range [1, 234997] = full domain

  Extending from guards[0] to all guards adds more skip checks per block but
  NONE of the additional guards' key columns have narrow-enough guard ranges
  combined with sufficient block locality to skip any blocks.

3. When Block Skip Would Help
------------------------------
  Block skip is effective when:
  - The probe column IS the table's PK (perfect locality: block i = rows [2048i, 2048(i+1)))
  - AND the guard range is narrow (<50% of domain)

  Example: scanning title (2.5M rows) with a guard on title.id from an HT
  with a narrow key range (e.g., title.id in [100000, 200000]). Then blocks
  outside this range (>95% of blocks) would be skipped. But title steps are
  not the bottleneck (~7ms vs 200ms for cast_info).

