OPT-6: Inline String Comparison Functions — Analysis (DROPPED)
=================================================================
Date: 2026-07-31

Summary: No measurable benefit for JOB. perf profiling confirms all target
functions are at 0% in the profile. Specialized LIKE codegen already avoids
calling aqp_like_match. Decision: DROP this optimization.

1. LIKE Pattern Classification in Top JOB Queries
--------------------------------------------------
  Pattern                   Kind              Codegen
  %(producer)% (10c)        LIKE_CONTAINS     inline memchr+memcmp loop
  %a% (7c)                  LIKE_CONTAINS     inline memchr+memcmp loop
  A% (7c)                   LIKE_PREFIX       inline length+memcmp
  %B% (17f)                 LIKE_CONTAINS     inline memchr+memcmp loop

  None of these call aqp_like_match, aqp_str_eq, or aqp_like_match_segments.

2. IN-List Handling
--------------------
  ci.note IN ('(voice)',...) (9d, 19d) calls aqp_in_len_str externally.
  The function uses a length-bucketed directory: most rows short-circuit
  when slen doesn't match any IN-value length (dir[b] == dir[b+1]).

  Inlining would let LLVM:
  - Specialize for the known cap value
  - Hoist dir[] loads out of the row loop (constant across all rows)
  - Eliminate ~2-3ns call/return overhead per row

  Estimated saving: 36M × 2ns / 6 workers = ~12ms out of ~103ms = ~12%
  But only for 9d/19d (2 of top 7 queries).

3. What the Description Got Wrong
-----------------------------------
  - "1.05-1.1x on LIKE-heavy queries (10c, 9d)": 10c uses LIKE_CONTAINS
    which is already fully inlined. No aqp_ call to optimize.
  - "aqp_like_match (lines 26-52)": never called in top queries (no
    LIKE_COMPLEX patterns).
  - "aqp_like_match_segments (lines 177-210)": never called in top queries
    (no LIKE_MULTI_SEGMENT patterns).
  - The real target should be aqp_in_len_str for IN-lists, not aqp_like_match.

4. Timing Breakdown
--------------------
  9d: total=117ms, bottleneck subquery[13]=103ms (cast_info 36M scan)
  10c: total=155ms, bottleneck subquery[13]=140ms (cast_info 36M scan)
  In both cases the bottleneck is dominated by multi-probe directory loads,
  not string function calls.

5. perf Profiling (verified 2026-07-31)
----------------------------------------
  9d (ci.note IN (...)):
    aqp_in_len_str: 0.00% — NOT VISIBLE in profile
    Top symbols: HT Finalize (11.5%), JIT morsel code (2.6%, 2.1%, 1.8%,...),
    memset (5.4%), memcmp (1.2%)

  10c (ci.note LIKE '%(producer)%'):
    aqp_like_match: 0.00% — NOT CALLED (LIKE_CONTAINS inline codegen used)
    __memchr_avx2: 9.57% (inline LIKE_CONTAINS loop)
    __memcmp_avx2: 3.81%
    JIT morsel code: 14.0%, 8.4%, 3.0%,...

6. TPDE vs LLVM Comparison (jit-cache=full, cached iterations)
---------------------------------------------------------------
  9d TPDE:  bottleneck=105.5ms  total=115.0ms
  9d LLVM:  bottleneck= 82.0ms  total= 89.9ms
  Gap: 22% — general LLVM O2 code quality, not string-specific.
  Inlining aqp_in_len_str would NOT close this gap (0% in profile).

7. Conclusion
--------------
  OPT-6 provides ZERO measurable benefit for JOB with query-JIT.
  No config benefits (TPDE, LLVM, template-cache, full-cache).
  The specialized LIKE codegen (ClassifyLikePatternEx) already avoids
  calling the external aqp_ string functions for all patterns in JOB.

OPT-8: SimplifyCFG Before TPDE — IMPLEMENTED
=============================================
Date: 2026-07-31
Config: DuckDB, JOB, query-jit, TPDE, single-run-template, no-spec

Summary: Running LLVM SimplifyCFG pass before TPDE backend gives -6.5% wall time
(topdown) / -5.7% (node-based). This is the ONLY pass worth adding — all other
LLVM O3 passes and combinations are net-negative on wall time. Spec-recompile
with any pass configuration is also net-negative.
Decision: IMPLEMENT SimplifyCFG-only for TPDE. No further passes viable.

1. Backend Code Quality Comparison (cache-full, execution only)
---------------------------------------------------------------
  LLVM O2:  3675ms (baseline — 4 mid-end passes + SelectionDAG ISel)
  TPDE:     4732ms (+29% — no mid-end passes, single-pass regalloc)
  FastISel:  5034ms (+37% — no mid-end passes, LLVM fast ISel)

  TPDE is 6.0% faster than FastISel. The 29% gap vs LLVM O2 comes
  entirely from the 4 skipped mid-end passes.

2. SimplifyCFG-only results (all 113 JOB queries, cache-tmpl, no-spec)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Config                        Topdown (ms)   vs baseline   Node-based (ms)   vs baseline
  TPDE baseline (no passes)     5039           —             5469              —
  TPDE + SimplifyCFG            4710           -6.5%         5156              -5.7%
  TPDE + InstCombine+SimplifyCFG 5152          +2.2%         —                 —
  TPDE + All 4 passes           5798           +15.1%        —                 —
  LLVM O2 (all 4 + SelectionDAG) 10386         +106%         —                 —

  Topdown: 80 improved, 16 worsened (max 3.4ms), 17 neutral.
  Node-based: 75 improved, 14 worsened.
  Stability: 3 runs gave 6.2%, 6.5%, 6.6% (mean 6.4% +/- 0.2%).
  Correctness: 113/113 PASS on both Config A (node-based) and Config B (topdown).

3. Per-pass analysis (16b topdown, result subquery, iter3)
----------------------------------------------------------
  Pass config              opt(ms)  step2(ms)  step3(ms)  exec(ms)  ROI
  No passes                0        66.5       43.4       109.9     —
  SimplifyCFG only         0.25     55.4       33.4       89.4      84x
  InstCombine only         1.5      64.3       42.8       107.0     1.9x
  GVN only                 1.4      68.2       42.1       110.2     negative
  InstCombine+SimplifyCFG  1.7      53.5       33.0       86.5      14x
  All 4 passes             3.1      53.3       33.4       86.7      7.5x

  SimplifyCFG: cheapest pass (0.25ms), saves 21ms exec. Best ROI by far.
  GVN alone: HURTS (step2 +1.7ms). Restructures IR without removing blocks.
  InstCombine: 1.5ms cost for 2.8ms exec savings. Net marginal.

4. Why SimplifyCFG alone is effective
-------------------------------------
  The query-JIT codegen (CompileQuerySteps) produces structurally eliminable
  basic blocks from:
  - Validity check diamonds for non-nullable columns (src_valid_i1, 9603-9618)
  - Null check chains for block_stats/user_data pointers (9220-9260)
  - Single-predecessor jump-through blocks from per-op codegen pattern

  SimplifyCFG merges these blocks, reducing jumps and improving TPDE's
  single-pass register allocator (fewer live ranges cross block boundaries).

5. All LLVM O3 passes evaluated (9 passes, 13 combinations, LLVM 20)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
  Measured on all 113 JOB queries (topdown cache-tmpl):

  Config                          Wall (ms)   vs SimplifyCFG
  S (SimplifyCFG only)            4710        baseline
  DS (ADCE+SimplifyCFG)           4824        +2.4%
  PS (SCCP+SimplifyCFG)           4877        +3.5%
  PDS (SCCP+ADCE+SimplifyCFG)    4926        +4.6%
  IS (InstCombine+SimplifyCFG)    5152        +9.4%
  IRGS (all 4 original)           5798        +23.1%

  Per-pass deltas (16b, measured as delta vs SimplifyCFG alone):
  Pass                  compile cost(ms)   exec savings(ms)   ROI
  ADCE                  0.15               4.5                30x
  SCCP                  0.07               3.0                43x
  EarlyCSE              0.16               -1.1               negative
  JumpThreading         0.10               0.4                4x
  CorrelatedValueProp   0.31               1.4                4.5x
  BDCE                  0.0                0.7                high
  InstCombine           1.24               3.7                3x
  GVN                   1.4                -1.7               negative

  ADCE had best single-query ROI (30x on 16b) but on full benchmark was +2.4%
  worse. Verified by weight-bucketed analysis: ADCE is worse even for queries
  >100ms wall time (heavy: +0.8ms, light: +112.7ms).

  Fundamental constraint: any pass costing >0.3ms/subquery x ~450 subqueries =
  135ms overhead, while marginal exec savings beyond SimplifyCFG total <50ms
  across all 113 queries (concentrated in ~15 heavy queries).

6. Guard hash reuse (inlining guard hash into probe) — NOT VIABLE
-----------------------------------------------------------------
  Analytical trace of control flow:
  Case A (small HT <=64K, membership=true): hash duplicated on <=NumEntries
  rows. For HT3 in 16b (24K entries): 24K x ~2ns = 0.05ms. Negligible.
  Case B (large HT >64K, membership=false): no guard hash computed. Zero dup.

7. Runtime constants (Cosense-style) + SimplifyCFG — NOT VIABLE
---------------------------------------------------------------
  Reference: Cosense (CC'24, Mu et al.) substitutes sensor-range constants
  into LLVM IR, resolves branches to true/false, lets SimplifyCFG clean up.
  Achieves 1.18-1.70x speedup on sensor code.

  Our case differs: the specializable branches (g_mem_ok, has_user, has_bstats)
  are all loop-invariant (checked once per morsel, not per row). Branch
  prediction already handles them at ~0 runtime cost. Benefit is purely
  indirect (code size reduction -> better TPDE regalloc). Estimated <1%
  additional improvement, not worth the plumbing complexity.

  Runtime info available from split execution (HT NumEntries, Key0Min/Max,
  temp cardinalities) are exact values from completed subqueries. But the
  branches they control are loop-invariant, making constant substitution
  a second-order optimization at best.

8. Spec-recompile + LLVM passes — NOT VIABLE
---------------------------------------------
  Spec-recompile overlaps compilation with prior subquery execution on a
  background thread (jit_compile_pool_, single worker).

  Measured statistics (topdown, 113 queries, 1 iteration):
  - Total speculations: 684 (102 queries)
  - Hit rate: 100% (342 hits, 0 SQL misses, 0 cardinality misses)
  - Not-ready rate: 50% (342 events — bg compile didn't finish in time)
  - Total main-thread stall: 4,588ms (avg 13.4ms per not-ready event)

  Root cause: bg thread runs DuckDB Parse+Plan+Optimize (~15-20ms/subquery)
  before JIT compilation. Changing bg JIT backend (LLVM O2 vs TPDE+SimplifyCFG)
  had zero effect on stall time (29ms both cases for 16b).

  Results (topdown cache-tmpl, all 113 queries):
  Config                              Wall (ms)  vs SCFG no-spec
  TPDE+SimplifyCFG no-spec            4710       baseline
  TPDE+SimplifyCFG spec-rec (TPDE bg) 9161       +94.5%
  TPDE+SimplifyCFG spec-rec (LLVM bg) 9189       +95.1%

  Spec-recompile fails because small subqueries (1-10ms exec) can't hide
  the 20ms DuckDB Prepare cost.

9. Code changes
----------------
  File: src/jit/ir_to_llvm.cpp
  - OptimiseModule: added FastCompileBackend param. TPDE gets SimplifyCFG only,
    OFF (LLVM) gets all 4 passes (unchanged), FastISel skips all (unchanged).
  - Constructor: skip_opt_ = debug || fast == FASTISEL (was: fast != OFF).
    TPDE now runs passes (skip_opt_=false).
  - All 11 call sites: pass fast_mode_ through.
  - Cache key: already encodes fast_mode_ (int) + skip_opt_ flag ("n"/"o"),
    which uniquely identifies the pass configuration. No change needed.

10. Conclusion
--------------
  SimplifyCFG is the ONLY pass that provides net wall-time improvement before
  TPDE on the JOB benchmark. No other LLVM O3 pass, combination of passes,
  runtime constant specialization, or spec-recompile configuration can beat
  TPDE+SimplifyCFG on wall time. The fundamental constraint is that most JOB
  queries are light (<20ms wall time), making any additional compile cost
  beyond SimplifyCFG's 0.25ms/subquery net-negative.

=================================================================
OPT-2a: CSR-Driven Scan Inversion — IMPLEMENTED AND DROPPED
=================================================================
Date: 2026-07-31 to 2026-08-01
Config: DuckDB, JOB, query-jit, TPDE, single-run-template, no-spec

Summary: CSR morsel provides 15-29% per-step improvement on eligible steps,
but 500MB CSR index memory pressure causes 5-6s regression across all 113
queries. NET NEGATIVE. Code reverted.

1. Concept
----------
Instead of scanning a large FK table (cast_info, 36M rows) and probing a
small HT (68K entries), iterate the HT entries and use a pre-built CSR index
to look up only the matching FK rows. For 16b step 2: 36M → ~1.5M rows
(~24x reduction).

CSR indexes map PK value → FK row index ranges:
  CSR("cast_info","movie_id").Lookup(title_id) → [begin, end) in col_idx
  col_idx[i] = cast_info row index where movie_id == title_id

2. Implementation
-----------------
Dual-morsel approach: compile both normal and CSR morsel per eligible step.
Runtime gate in entry function: qjit_ht_entries(driving_ht) * 100 < src_nrows.
If gate passes, call qjit_csr_build_keys (extracts key0 + row pointers from
driving HT) then dispatch CSR morsel; else dispatch normal morsel.

CSR morsel structure: outer loop over HT entries [begin, end), inner loop
over CSR-retrieved source rows. Driving probe skipped (live_row set directly
from HT entry). Filters and secondary probes execute normally.

Files modified: 15 files (~1100 lines added). Both DuckDB and PG adapters.
Correctness: 113/113 PASS on Config A and Config B.

3. Measured Results
-------------------
  Per-step (16b step 2, cast_info scan, result subquery):
    Normal morsel:  pfor = 41ms
    CSR morsel:     pfor = 29-35ms (15-29% improvement)
    CSR rows processed: ~1.5M (from 68K HT entries via CSR lookup)

  Whole benchmark (113 queries, node-based TPDE cache-tmpl):
    Baseline (no CSR):        exe=4.37s  total=5.18s
    CSR + lazy loading:       exe=9.99s  total=10.82s  (+109% regression)
    CSR + pre-loaded cache:   exe=10.17s total=11.02s  (+113% regression)
    CSR disabled (100M gate): exe=4.47s  total=5.29s   (baseline restored)

4. Root Cause of Regression
----------------------------
Loading all 19 CSR indexes (~500MB for IMDB) into DRAM — whether eagerly
at startup or lazily on first use — evicts FlatTable column data (~3.7GB)
from the OS page cache. Confirmed by:
  a) CSR enabled but annotation disabled (no morsel gen, no build_keys):
     exe=6.25s (+43%) — regression from CSR data in memory alone
  b) CSR loading disabled entirely: exe=4.47s — baseline restored
  c) The regression persists even when CSR morsel never executes (100x gate
     rejects all steps), confirming the cost is memory pressure not execution

5. First Attempt Failure (Instrumentation Bug)
----------------------------------------------
First implementation appeared to show "zero speedup" (CSR morsel = normal
morsel = 41ms). This was wrong — the CSR morsel was never executing.

Root cause: fprintf traces added to ir_to_llvm.cpp (compiled into static
library libaqp_jit_lib.a) were silently stripped by the clang-20 -O3
optimizer + ccache, even with CCACHE_DISABLE=1. Format strings were absent
from the object file despite confirmed compilation.

Fixed in re-implementation by using runtime row counters in QjitCSRDesc
(incremented by CSR morsel, checked in executor after fn returns) and
placing all traces in query_jit_runtime.cpp (separate translation unit,
not in the static library).

6. CSR Index Memory Usage (19 FK relationships from fkeys.sql)
--------------------------------------------------------------
  FK Column                    row_ptr (MB)  col_idx (MB)
  cast_info.movie_id           19            138
  cast_info.person_id          31            138
  cast_info.person_role_id     23             67
  cast_info.role_id             0            138
  movie_info.movie_id          19             56
  movie_keyword.movie_id       19             17
  movie_companies.movie_id     19              9
  movie_companies.company_id    1              9
  aka_name.person_id           31              3
  ...others (small)             ~5            ~15
  Total:                       ~170           ~590  = ~760 MB

7. Why Per-Query CSR Building Would Help
-----------------------------------------
16b only needs CSR("cast_info","movie_id"): row_ptr=19MB + col_idx=138MB = 157MB.
This is 5x less than loading all 19 indexes (760MB). The memory pressure
regression should scale linearly with CSR size.

8. Potential on Other Benchmarks
---------------------------------
JOB is uniquely memory-bound: 0/113 queries have SUM/AVG/arithmetic, only MIN
aggregates. Per-row compute is ~2ns (hash + compare), per-row memory is ~20-40ns.
CSR row reduction saves mostly memory work, which is offset by random access cost.

TPC-H: 97% queries have SUM/AVG + arithmetic (price * (1-discount) * (1+tax)).
Per-row compute is ~15-50ns. CSR row reduction would save 10x × 30ns = 300ns/row
of compute, far exceeding the ~40ns random access penalty.

DSB/TPC-DS: complex WHERE clauses, BETWEEN ranges, OR groups. Per-row compute
is ~10-30ns. CSR reduction would also be net positive.

9. Verified Assumptions
------------------------
- HT key0 = raw FK/PK int32 value (sign-extended i64). Confirmed at
  ir_to_llvm.cpp:9669 (LoadI32 + SExt), query_jit_runtime.cpp:208 (memcpy).
- CSRIndex::Lookup(int32_t pk_val) matches HT key0 after truncation.
- CSR API: public members row_ptr/col_idx, Lookup() method. No Offsets()/
  ColIdx()/MaxPK() methods (CLAUDE.md had wrong API reference).
- BuildExecutionSteps has NO access to StoragePlan — detection must happen
  in ResolveQjitSources or adapter AnnotateCSRSteps.
- Step-timing finalize has off-by-one (scheduler.cpp:128-130).

OPT-2b: HT Finalize Optimization — DROPPED
=================================================================
Date: 2026-08-01
Config: DuckDB, JOB, query-jit, TPDE, single-run-template, no-spec, node-based

Summary: All proposed finalize optimizations are net-negative or no-op.
calloc (the most promising) increases finalize by +20% (+121ms) because
explicit memset serves as TLB warmup for the prefetch ring buffer.

1. Baseline Measurement
-----------------------
  Total wall time: 5147ms (avg iter 6-15, 113 queries)
  Total HT finalize: 611ms (11.9% of wall time)
  864 HTs total, 110 large (>100K entries) = 541ms (88% of finalize)
  Actual load factor (n/dir_size): 0.25-0.50 (avg 0.36)

  Cost breakdown for n=3.7M, dir=8M (64MB):
    Parallel memset (12 workers): <0.5ms
    Entry linking (CAS + prefetch ring): ~17-20ms
    Memset is <5% of finalize cost; linking dominates.

2. calloc Instead of new+memset — IMPLEMENTED AND REVERTED
-----------------------------------------------------------
  Change: Replace `new atomic<uintptr_t>[dir_size]` + explicit memset with
  `calloc(dir_size, sizeof(atomic<uintptr_t>))` + FreeDeleter on unique_ptr.
  Removes both parallel and serial memset code paths.

  Microbenchmark (synthetic, 64MB dir, 3.7M entries, 12 threads):
    new+parallel_memset+parallel_link: 48.9ms
    calloc+parallel_link: 33.7ms (31% faster)

  Real benchmark (113 JOB queries, node-based TPDE cache-tmpl):
    Finalize: 611ms → 732ms (+121ms, +20%)
    Wall time: 5147ms → 5159ms (+12ms, +0.2%)

  Root cause of regression: The Finalize-internal prefetch ring buffer
  (query_jit_runtime.cpp:185-221, kPFDist=16) requires mapped pages to work.
  x86 spec: __builtin_prefetch is silently dropped for unmapped pages.

  With new+memset (baseline):
  (a) memset touches every page → pages mapped, in TLB
  (b) Prefetch 16 entries ahead → succeeds → brings cache line from DRAM to L1
  (c) CAS hits warm cache line → ~5-10ns per operation

  With calloc:
  (a) calloc returns zero-mapped pages via kernel's shared zero page (COW)
  (b) Prefetch 16 entries ahead → SILENTLY DROPPED (page not yet faulted)
  (c) CAS load → minor page fault (~1μs), CAS store → COW fault (~1μs)
  (d) No latency hiding for first access to each of 16K pages (64MB / 4KB)

  The synthetic microbenchmark missed this because it used simple stores
  WITHOUT a prefetch ring buffer. Without prefetch, there's no latency
  hiding to lose, so calloc genuinely saved one memory pass. But in the
  real code, killing prefetch effectiveness costs far more than the <0.5ms
  parallel memset it replaces.

  Correctness: 113/113 PASS on Config A and Config B (before revert).

3. Other Techniques — NOT TESTED (Analysis Only)
-------------------------------------------------
  Reduce overallocation (2x → 1.5x):
    Parallel memset savings: <1ms total across 113 queries.
    Probe-side cost: higher load (avg 0.55 vs 0.36) → ~15% longer chains
    → LLC-miss regression on ~200M probe rows >> 1ms memset savings.
    Verdict: NET NEGATIVE without testing.

  madvise(MADV_HUGEPAGE):
    THP already `[always]` on this system. No-op.

  Pre-size from prior-subquery cardinality:
    Current overallocation (2-4x) already bounded. Prior cardinality is
    only an upper bound (not tight). No benefit.

4. Conclusion
--------------
  HT Finalize (611ms, 11.9% of wall time) cannot be cheaply optimized.
  The prefetch ring buffer already hides DRAM latency for directory access.
  The explicit memset phase doubles as TLB warmup. The remaining cost is
  inherent: 3.7M CAS operations with random directory access patterns.

=================================================================
OPT-3: Probe-Side Prefetching — Re-evaluation (2026-08-01)
=================================================================
Config: DuckDB, JOB, query-jit, TPDE, single-run-template, no-spec

Summary: Row-ahead prefetch CONFIRMED NOT VIABLE. Batch prefetch viable
in microbenchmark (7%) but requires ROF (which is also dead). DROPPED.

1. Re-analysis: Why Row-Ahead Prefetch Truly Fails
---------------------------------------------------
  The original log blamed per-row branch overhead in TPDE code. Re-analysis
  with microbenchmarks shows the real reason is deeper:

  a) Row-ahead prefetch (split loop, zero branches, precomputed hashes) is
     STILL neutral-to-negative even on a single 64MB HT with random keys.
     The CPU's out-of-order window (168 insns on Coffee Lake, ~42ns at 4 IPC)
     already overlaps chain-walk computation with the next dir load.

  b) Unconditional prefetch on small HTs (L1-resident, <2MB dir) adds +46%
     overhead from wasted hash computation + prefetch port contention.

  c) Multi-HT realistic scenario (3 HTs: 85K + 772K + 3.7M entries):
     No prefetch:          215.4 ms (71.8 ns/row)
     Prefetch large only:  227.8 ms (75.9 ns/row) — WORSE
     Prefetch all:         231.0 ms (77.0 ns/row) — WORSE

2. Batch Prefetch (Phase 1/Phase 2) — Viable in Microbenchmark
--------------------------------------------------------------
  Phase 1: hash + prefetch for B rows. Phase 2: probe those B rows.
  Single 64MB HT:  no_pf=109.3ms  batch_B=64=87.6ms (20% faster)
  3-HT scenario:   no_pf=215.4ms  batch_B=32=200.1ms (7.1% faster)

  But batch prefetch requires ROF-style two-phase structure in query-JIT.
  ROF was tested and is dead (see OPT-5 below). So batch prefetch is
  not implementable without ROF.

3. HT Sizes for Top Queries (topdown, verified 2026-08-01)
----------------------------------------------------------
  16b: 85K(2MB) + 1(tiny) + 42K(1MB) + 68K(2MB) + 773K(16MB) + 68K(2MB) + 3.7M(64MB)
  8c:  85K(2MB) + 773K(16MB) + 1(tiny) + 2.3M(64MB) + 2.5M(64MB) + 2.5M(64MB)

=================================================================
OPT-5: ROF (Reorder-Filter) for TPDE — Re-evaluation (2026-08-01)
=================================================================
Config: DuckDB, JOB, query-jit, TPDE, single-run-template, no-spec

Summary: Unguarded ROF +13.4% regression. Guarded ROF within noise
(-0.21%). DROPPED.

1. Unguarded ROF (all steps)
----------------------------
  Removed TPDE + SIMD gates from ROF decision (ir_to_llvm.cpp:9274).
  Correctness: 113/113 PASS on topdown and node-based.
  Performance: +13.4% total regression (4726ms → 5357ms topdown).
  90 queries worse, 15 better, 8 neutral.

  Worst regressions: small queries (3a: +118%, 15a: +109%, 5c: +92%).
  Phase 1 writes (row_idx, hash) to stack buffers for nearly every row
  (guard pass rate 97-100%), then Phase 2 reads them back.

  Best improvements: 10c (-13.6%), 18c (-11.7%), 10b (-21%) — queries
  where Phase 1 LIKE/IN filters on cast_info reject rows before probing.

2. Guarded ROF (pre-probe Filter + >=2 post-filter Probes)
----------------------------------------------------------
  Enable ROF only for steps with a pre-probe Filter AND >=2 probes.
  Single run:    -0.8% topdown, -1.1% node-based
  3-run average: -0.21% topdown (4732ms → 4722ms, stdev 28ms)
  Verdict: within noise.

3. Hash Reuse Detail
---------------------
  ROF saves 1 Murmur hash per qualifying row (for rof_first_probe only;
  other probes recompute). But adds 2 buffer stores + 2 buffer loads
  (~5ns), roughly canceling the ~5ns Murmur savings. Non-membership
  guards (large HTs >64K) do NOT compute hash — only range check.
  The real benefit is purely from filtering rows before probing.

4. OPT-3 + OPT-5 Combined
---------------------------
  Batch prefetch (7% microbenchmark) requires restructuring ROF into
  batched processing (Phase 1 for B rows → Phase 2 for B rows).
  Complex code change for ~5-10ms additional gain on top of marginal
  guarded ROF. Not viable.

=================================================================
Exhaustive Review of Runtime-Guided Optimizations (2026-08-01)
=================================================================

All OPTs re-analyzed. Three additional SOTA techniques investigated.

| OPT   | Technique                  | Verdict | Why                                         |
|-------|----------------------------|---------|---------------------------------------------|
| 1     | Dense array joins          | DEAD    | Memory-bound; array spans full PK domain    |
| 2a    | CSR scan inversion         | DEAD    | 500MB CSR evicts page cache                 |
| 2b    | HT finalize (calloc)       | DEAD    | Kills prefetch ring TLB warmup              |
| 3     | Row-ahead prefetch         | DEAD    | CPU OoO already hides DRAM latency          |
| 4     | Block skip on FK           | DEAD    | No block locality + wide guard ranges       |
| 5     | ROF for TPDE               | DEAD    | 97% guard pass → buffer overhead wasted     |
| 5g    | Guarded ROF                | NOISE   | -0.21% (±0.6%), within stdev                |
| 6     | Inline string cmp          | DEAD    | Target functions never called in JOB        |
| 8     | SimplifyCFG                | DONE    | -6.5% topdown, implemented (commit 164ecac) |
| NEW-A | Hash dedup across probes   | DEAD    | CPU pipelines ALU behind mem latency        |
| NEW-B | Probe reorder by select.   | DEAD    | <1% JOB/DSB; TPDE regresses 17b/17c        |
| NEW-C | Batch prefetch (AMAC)      | DEAD    | Only works with ROF (which is dead)          |

NEW-A: Hash Deduplication Across Probes
----------------------------------------
  Multiple probes sharing the same join key (e.g., 3 probes on ci.movie_id
  in 16b) each recompute Murmur hash independently. TPDE cannot CSE them.
  Microbenchmark with noinline Murmur: 7.186ms (5 calls) vs 6.814ms (2
  calls) = 5.2% ALU savings. But with inline Murmur at -O1 (simulating
  TPDE): 5.207ms vs 5.225ms = 0% savings. The redundant hash ALU fills
  pipeline bubbles while waiting for memory (same root cause as OPT-3).

NEW-B: Probe Reordering by Selectivity
----------------------------------------
  Sort probes by ascending min(build_table_cardinalities). Most selective
  probe first eliminates rows via inner-join before expensive large-HT
  probes. Implemented, tested extensively across multiple configurations:

  Implementation: estimated_entries on QjitHtDesc, PlanJoinFilterPushdown
  sorts independent probes (all_src keys), block_skip_guard preserves
  original blockskip column. BuildExecutionSteps accepts table_card map.

  Best result (A/B, topdown TPDE cache-tmpl):
    JOB:    -0.59% (4746→4718ms). 42 better, 17 worse.
    DSB-10: variable ±0.82% across runs (noise, 22 queries).
    17b/17c: +14-17ms regression (TPDE CFG sensitivity).

  Regression root cause: TPDE sensitivity to CFG topology. Swapping 2
  probes changes basic block connections → TPDE's single-pass register
  allocator produces different spill/reload patterns → ~15ms slower
  machine code for 17b/17c specifically.

  Mitigation attempts (all failed):
  - >=3 probes guard: protects 17b/17c but kills benefit (most JOB
    steps have only 2 probes)
  - source_table cardinality instead of min_card: overestimates base
    tables (unfiltered row_count)
  - Splitter filtered cardinalities (name-based lookup): table index
    mismatch across subqueries (DuckDB re-binds with new indices)
  - 8x cardinality ratio guard: doesn't protect 17c (splitter gives
    28x ratio for HTs that actually have similar real entries)
  - blockskip_col preservation (block_skip_guard field): regression
    persists (+15ms) — issue is CFG topology, not block stats column
  - RPO block canonicalization (LLVM ReversePostOrderTraversal):
    regression persists (+15ms) — issue is CFG edges, not block order

  Why dropped: <1% net improvement on JOB and DSB-10, within noise.
  DSB-100 same templates as DSB-10 (same probe structure, same %).
  TPC-H not applicable (query-JIT lacks arithmetic expression support).
  TPDE fix requires weeks-months (16K+ lines core, register allocator).

  Code fully reverted — no probe reordering in codebase.

NEW-C: AMAC / Group Prefetch
-----------------------------
  Interleave dir loads across probes for the same row (issue all prefetches
  at once, then do all chain walks). Requires ROF-style batching to
  accumulate multiple rows' hashes before probing. Dead because ROF is dead.

Why JOB is Resistant to Runtime-Guided Optimization
----------------------------------------------------
  The bottleneck (36M-row cast_info scan with 3-5 random HT probes into
  16-64MB directories) is dominated by DRAM latency (~80ns per probe).
  The CPU's out-of-order window (168 insns, ~42ns at 4 IPC) already
  overlaps ALU work (hash, guards, filters) with memory accesses.
  Software techniques to hide latency (prefetch, batching, hash dedup)
  are redundant because the hardware is already doing it.

  The only way to reduce wall time is to reduce the NUMBER of rows or
  probes, not their per-row cost. This points to:
  - Better split strategies (smaller subqueries)
  - Fundamentally different execution models (vectorized, partitioned)
  - Hardware with larger L3 cache (more HT directories fit in cache)


OPT-7: Bloom Filter Block-Skip for Query-JIT — INVESTIGATED AND DROPPED
=========================================================================
Date: 2026-08-05
Config: DuckDB, Topdown, Query-JIT, TPDE, Cache-Tmpl, No-Spec, JOB 113 queries

Goal: Use runtime BF statistics from temp tables to skip 2048-row blocks
during query-jit compiled scans. Two approaches tested.

V1: Zero-Codegen Cross-Sub-Plan BF Sorted Keys
-----------------------------------------------
Approach: Extract sorted distinct INT32 keys from temp-table BFs during
BuildBloomFilterFromQjitTable. For each step scanning a base table, match
BFs by table name, compute per-block {min,max} for the BF target column
via GetBlockStats, mark blocks with no matching key as {INT32_MAX, INT32_MIN}
in a copy of the guard's block_stats. Zero ir_to_llvm.cpp changes.

Result: -35ms (-0.8%) — within noise.

Root cause of limited benefit:
  - cast_info (36M rows, the bottleneck table) is NEVER a BF target in
    topdown split. It is scanned in early sub-queries and becomes a temp
    table. BFs are built from temp tables and target base tables in LATER
    sub-queries (title, char_name, etc.).
  - Tables that DO get BF block-skip (title 2.5M, char_name 3.1M, name
    4.2M) are small — skipping 90%+ of their blocks saves only a few ms.
  - 125 BF-SKIP events across 113 queries, 40.8% blocks skipped overall,
    but concentrated on small tables.

V2: Sorted cast_info + Multi-Guard Block Skip (Codegen)
-------------------------------------------------------
Approach: Sort cast_info by movie_id (ORDER BY movie_id, id in storage
loading) so movie_id per-block ranges become narrow (~143 span vs 2.5M
unsorted). Add bf_block_stats as independent second block-skip channel in
QjitUserData (field 3). In codegen, for steps with bf_guard_idx >= 0,
load bf_block_stats and check block range against the matching guard's
HT key0 range. Skip block if EITHER guard says skip.

This revives OPT-4 (Multi-Key Block Skip, dropped because unsorted columns
have 99% block span). After sorting, movie_id block spans become ~143.

Result: +16ms (+0.3%) — slight regression, no improvement.

Root cause:
  - 57 cast_info steps across JOB have guards. block_skip_col (guards[0]
    key-0) is movie_id in SOME queries but person_id or id in MOST.
  - Sorting by movie_id DESTROYS id and person_id block locality:
      id:        dense_pk (perfect) → random (~99% span)
      person_id: 27.8% span → ~99% span
    This makes the EXISTING guard-based block-skip worse for non-movie_id
    guard steps.
  - bf_block_skip_col only helps the 12/57 two-guard steps where guard[1]
    is on movie_id. For the 45 single-guard steps where the guard is NOT
    movie_id, no benefit.
  - Net: sorting hurts more steps than bf_block_skip helps.
  - Also: codegen branches for bf_stats null-check add per-block overhead
    even when bf_block_stats is null (most steps).

Decision: DROPPED. Both approaches provide negligible or negative benefit.
The fundamental constraint is that topdown split processes cast_info early,
so neither cross-sub-plan BFs nor within-sub-query HT keys can effectively
filter cast_info block scans.

Previously reverted commit f008485 ("implement block-level skip for temp
table probe") was also investigated and dropped — it had a stale cache bug
(block_stats_ keyed by freed heap address) causing non-deterministic
correctness failures, and the "speedup" it showed was entirely from
incorrect block-skipping (fewer rows = faster but wrong results). With
correct stats, temp-table block-skip provides 0% benefit (same root cause
as OPT-4: unsorted columns have wide block ranges).
